{"id":"gen-1790979286-yz6P5VfI0Xcz943P4xV9","object":"chat.completion","created":1790979286,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\n        \"c1\",\n        \"c2\",\n        \"c3\",\n        \"c4\",\n        \"c5\",\n        \"c6\",\n        \"c7\",\n        \"c8\",\n        \"c9\",\n        \"c10\",\n        \"c11\",\n        \"c12\",\n        \"c13\",\n        \"c14\",\n        \"c15\",\n        \"c16\",\n        \"c17\",\n        \"c18\",\n        \"c19\",\n        \"c20\"\n      ],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      },\n      \"candidate_tuples\": [\n        [0,1,3,2]\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet. The envelope template is given with fields. We need to produce a JSON envelope matching the template. The envelope includes \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\", etc. The payload contains a report with assessment, basis_refs, target etc. The task is to independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19 (['j', 'm']). Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. No accepted peer proposal is available; report that state without inventing one.\n\nWe need to produce the envelope. The envelope template is given with placeholders? It includes a particular structure: \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\", \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\", \"payload\": {\"report\": {\"assessment\": \"supported\", \"basis_refs\": [...], \"target\": {...}} }, etc. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" But the envelope template doesn't have candidate tuples. Perhaps we need to include candidate tuples? Actually, the \"payload\" contains a \"report\" object; inside that we have \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". There's no candidate tuples in this envelope. But maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" But the envelope template given doesn't have candidate tuples. Possibly the \"payload\" also contains a \"candidate_tuples\" field? Or maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside the report? The problem description: \"Independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19 (['j', 'm']). Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nSo the envelope likely should include a \"candidate_tuples\" field somewhere. But the given envelope template does not have it. Perhaps the envelope template is just a skeleton, and we need to fill in the fields. The instructions say \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So we need to output a JSON that matches the template, but we may need to adjust fields based on the assessment. It says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to produce a JSON that includes those fields possibly. The template includes \"assessment\": \"supported\". But we might need to change to \"underdetermined\" or \"supported\". The basis_refs list is given. Should we edit it? Probably not needed. But we need to check the c4 component tuple enumeration against constraints and bridge c19.\n\nThus the primary task is to examine the c4 component tuple enumeration (i.e., the values for variables in component c4: m, n, o, p). The constraints c13-c16 involve those variables. c13: (m, n) allowed pairs: [ [0,1], [2,2], [0,3], [3,0], [2,1], [2,0] ]. c14: (n, o) allowed: [ [1,3], [3,2], [3,0], [0,1], [2,0], [0,3] ]. c15: (o, p) allowed: [ [3,2], [0,2], [3,3], [0,1], [2,1], [1,1] ]. c16: (p, m) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,3], [0,0], [1,2], [1,3], [2,3] ].\n\nAlso bridge constraints: c19 is a bridge between component c3 and c4, variables j and m. c19 allowed pairs: [ [3,0], [0,0] ]. So j (in component c3) and m (in component c4) must be either (3,0) or (0,0). So m is either 0 (if j=0) or 0 (if j=3) or maybe m=0 always? Actually both options have m=0. So m must be 0. So from bridge c19 we deduce m = 0.\n\nThus we need to see if the c4 component tuple enumeration (i.e., all possible assignments for m,n,o,p satisfying constraints c13-c16) is consistent with m=0 from bridge c19. The c4 component tuple enumeration presumably is the set of all tuples (m,n,o,p) that satisfy constraints c13-c16 (and maybe also other constraints linking to other components? c4 also appears in constraints c4 (d,a) and c20 (n,a) but those are separate. c4 component is m,n,o,p. c4 is also a constraint between d and a (c4: (d,a) allowed pairs). That is separate. The c4 component is distinct from constraint c4. The component c4 includes variables m,n,o,p.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all tuples (m,n,o,p) that satisfy c13-c16. Then check if any of those have m=0 as required by bridge c19. If all have m=0, then the enumeration is consistent. If some have m != 0, then there is a conflict.\n\nWe need to check if the enumeration is \"supported\" (i.e., consistent with constraints) or \"underdetermined\" (i.e., ambiguous). The instructions: \"Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined.\" So we need to assess whether the c4 component tuple enumeration is fully determined (i.e., unique) or underdetermined (multiple possibilities). Or maybe \"supported\" means the enumeration is consistent with constraints. \"Underdetermined\" means there are multiple possibilities. The phrasing \"supported or underdetermined\" suggests we need to choose between these two. Usually in CSP, \"supported\" means the enumeration is consistent and possibly unique? But \"underdetermined\" means there are multiple possibilities.\n\nWe need to \"independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19 (['j', 'm']).\" So we need to verify that the enumeration includes only tuples that satisfy c13-c16 and also satisfy the bridge constraint c19 (i.e., m=0). The enumeration is presumably given somewhere? But we have not been given any enumeration. The problem statement says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below.\" So we need to solve the CSP: find a complete assignment for a through p satisfying all constraints. Then we need to \"independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19\". So maybe we need to produce the enumeration for c4 component (i.e., the values of m,n,o,p) as part of solving the CSP. The envelope template includes \"assessment\" and \"basis_refs\". There's no \"candidate_tuples\". But maybe the envelope is expected to contain a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to include candidate tuples in the envelope. The envelope template is given but maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside the \"report\"? Or maybe inside \"payload\"? The template didn't show candidate tuples, but we can add them.\n\nBut the instructions say \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\" So we must output JSON that matches the template. The template is a JSON object with fields: \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\", \"schema_version\", \"sender\". Inside payload, there is \"report\" with fields \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". There's no candidate_tuples. So maybe the envelope is just a report of evidence, not including candidate tuples. The instruction about editing candidate tuples might refer to a different part of the protocol not shown. But we only have this envelope template. So perhaps we just need to output the envelope with assessment \"supported\" (or \"underdetermined\") and basis_refs as given. The candidate tuples might be part of the \"report\" but not shown. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope template is incomplete and we need to include candidate tuples. But the envelope template is the one we need to match. It doesn't have candidate tuples. So maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? But that would deviate from the template.\n\nWe need to interpret the instructions. The problem: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we must produce a JSON envelope that matches the template. The template includes fields. The envelope is a report of evidence. The \"assessment\" field currently is \"supported\". We might need to adjust to \"underdetermined\" if appropriate. The \"basis_refs\" list includes all constraints IDs. That seems fine.\n\nWe need to check the c4 component tuple enumeration against constraints. So we need to solve the CSP to find assignments for a-p. Then we need to check the enumeration for c4 component (m,n,o,p). We need to see if it's consistent with constraints and bridge c19. Then we need to produce a report with assessment \"supported\" if consistent and maybe unique? Or \"underdetermined\" if there are multiple possibilities. The instruction says \"Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined.\" So if we find an issue (e.g., enumeration violates constraints), we would use CHALLENGE. But we haven't been given any enumeration to check; we need to compute it. So we need to compute the enumeration and see if it is consistent.\n\nThus the primary task is to solve the CSP. Let's do that.\n\nWe have 16 variables: a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints:\n\n- c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [ [3,2], [2,3], [3,1], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1] ]\n- c2: (b,c) allowed: [ [2,1], [0,2], [3,2], [0,0], [3,1], [0,3] ]\n- c3: (c,d) allowed: [ [1,2], [2,2], [2,1], [3,2], [0,1], [0,0] ]\n- c4: (d,a) allowed: [ [2,3], [2,2], [3,0], [1,3], [3,3], [0,1] ]\n- c5: (e,f) allowed: [ [0,1], [1,1], [2,2], [0,2], [3,3], [2,1] ]\n- c6: (f,g) allowed: [ [1,3], [2,3], [0,1], [2,0], [1,1], [1,0] ]\n- c7: (g,h) allowed: [ [3,2], [3,1], [2,1], [1,0], [0,0], [3,0] ]\n- c8: (h,e) allowed: [ [2,0], [1,1], [2,2], [2,1], [0,3], [1,2] ]\n- c9: (i,j) allowed: [ [0,3], [0,2], [0,0], [1,1], [3,2], [2,3] ]\n- c10: (j,k) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,2], [1,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ]\n- c11: (k,l) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,3], [3,3], [2,0], [3,2], [1,0] ]\n- c12: (l,i) allowed: [ [2,0], [1,1], [3,3], [2,3], [2,2], [3,1] ]\n- c13: (m,n) allowed: [ [0,1], [2,2], [0,3], [3,0], [2,1], [2,0] ]\n- c14: (n,o) allowed: [ [1,3], [3,2], [3,0], [0,1], [2,0], [0,3] ]\n- c15: (o,p) allowed: [ [3,2], [0,2], [3,3], [0,1], [2,1], [1,1] ]\n- c16: (p,m) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,3], [0,0], [1,2], [1,3], [2,3] ]\n\nBridge constraints:\n- c17: (b,e) allowed: [ [2,0], [0,2] ]\n- c18: (f,i) allowed: [ [1,0], [2,1] ]\n- c19: (j,m) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,0] ]\n- c20: (n,a) allowed: [ [1,3], [1,0] ]\n\nThus we have a CSP with constraints linking variables across components.\n\nWe need to find a complete assignment for a through p satisfying all constraints. Then we need to check the c4 component tuple enumeration (i.e., the values of m,n,o,p) against constraints and bridge c19. So we need to solve the CSP, then examine the c4 component.\n\nWe need to produce a report of evidence. The envelope includes assessment and basis_refs. The candidate tuples might be part of the report? But the envelope template does not include candidate tuples. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" Possibly the envelope template is incomplete and we need to include candidate tuples in the \"payload\"? Let's read the envelope template fully:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\n        \"c1\",\n        \"c2\",\n        ...\n      ],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\"\n}\n\nThus the envelope is a report. There's no candidate tuples. So maybe the candidate tuples are part of the \"payload\" but not shown. Or maybe we need to output a different envelope that includes candidate tuples, but the instruction says \"matching the supplied template\". So we must match exactly the structure, but we can edit fields. The candidate tuples might be part of the \"report\" but not present in the template. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So perhaps the envelope template originally includes candidate tuples, but they are omitted in the snippet. But we have to follow the template. It might be that the \"payload\" field includes a \"report\" object that may have additional fields like \"candidate_tuples\". The template shows only \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". But we can add \"candidate_tuples\" if needed, but that would deviate from the template. However, the instruction says \"matching the supplied template\". That could be interpreted as the overall shape, not necessarily exact fields. But likely they want us to keep the same fields and not add extra ones. But the instruction about editing candidate tuples suggests there is a field for candidate tuples somewhere. Maybe the \"payload\" field also contains a \"candidate_tuples\" key, but it's not shown in the template because it's optional. The envelope template might be incomplete; we need to fill in the missing fields. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to produce a report that includes a \"candidate_tuples\" field. The \"assessment\" and \"basis_refs\" are already there. So we need to add \"candidate_tuples\". But the template does not show it, but we can include it as part of \"payload.report\". That would be a deviation but likely acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the \"payload\" is a map that includes \"report\" and also \"candidate_tuples\". The template might have omitted \"candidate_tuples\" for brevity. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to include candidate tuples. The \"assessment\" currently is \"supported\". We need to decide if it's \"supported\" or \"underdetermined\". Let's solve the CSP.\n\nWe have 16 variables and constraints. Let's attempt to solve manually or using reasoning. The constraints are fairly restrictive. Let's try to solve systematically.\n\nWe have constraints linking a,b,c,d in a cycle: c1 (a,b), c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). Also c20 (n,a) and c17 (b,e) and c18 (f,i) and c19 (j,m) and c5-c8 (e,f,g,h cycle) and c9-c12 (i,j,k,l cycle). So we have two cycles: (a,b,c,d) and (e,f,g,h) and (i,j,k,l) and (m,n,o,p) with bridge constraints linking them: c17 links b-e, c18 links f-i, c19 links j-m, c20 links n-a.\n\nThus we have a combined graph.\n\nWe need to find assignments for all variables. Let's denote domains: each variable ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nWe have constraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). So possible (a,b):\n- a=3,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=3,b=1\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=1,b=1\n- a=0,b=1\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed: (2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3). So possible (b,c):\n- b=2,c=1\n- b=0,c=2\n- b=3,c=2\n- b=0,c=0\n- b=3,c=1\n- b=0,c=3\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: (1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0). So possible (c,d):\n- c=1,d=2\n- c=2,d=2\n- c=2,d=1\n- c=3,d=2\n- c=0,d=1\n- c=0,d=0\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So possible (d,a):\n- d=2,a=3\n- d=2,a=2\n- d=3,a=0\n- d=1,a=3\n- d=3,a=3\n- d=0,a=1\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed: (1,3), (1,0). So possible (n,a):\n- n=1,a=3\n- n=1,a=0\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed: (2,0), (0,2). So possible (b,e):\n- b=2,e=0\n- b=0,e=2\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: (0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1). So possible (e,f):\n- e=0,f=1\n- e=1,f=1\n- e=2,f=2\n- e=0,f=2\n- e=3,f=3\n- e=2,f=1\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: (1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0). So possible (f,g):\n- f=1,g=3\n- f=2,g=3\n- f=0,g=1\n- f=2,g=0\n- f=1,g=1\n- f=1,g=0\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). So possible (g,h):\n- g=3,h=2\n- g=3,h=1\n- g=2,h=1\n- g=1,h=0\n- g=0,h=0\n- g=3,h=0\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So possible (h,e):\n- h=2,e=0\n- h=1,e=1\n- h=2,e=2\n- h=2,e=1\n- h=0,e=3\n- h=1,e=2\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: (1,0), (2,1). So possible (f,i):\n- f=1,i=0\n- f=2,i=1\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So possible (i,j):\n- i=0,j=3\n- i=0,j=2\n- i=0,j=0\n- i=1,j=1\n- i=3,j=2\n- i=2,j=3\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So possible (j,k):\n- j=3,k=0\n- j=0,k=2\n- j=1,k=0\n- j=0,k=1\n- j=1,k=3\n- j=3,k=2\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So possible (k,l):\n- k=0,l=2\n- k=1,l=3\n- k=3,l=3\n- k=2,l=0\n- k=3,l=2\n- k=1,l=0\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1). So possible (l,i):\n- l=2,i=0\n- l=1,i=1\n- l=3,i=3\n- l=2,i=3\n- l=2,i=2\n- l=3,i=1\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed: (3,0), (0,0). So possible (j,m):\n- j=3,m=0\n- j=0,m=0\n\nThus m must be 0, and j must be either 3 or 0.\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed: (0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0). Since m=0, we need (0,n) allowed. Allowed pairs with first component 0: (0,1) and (0,3). So n must be 1 or 3.\n\nThus n ∈ {1,3}.\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So for each possible n, we need allowed o.\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed: (3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1).\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: (2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). Since m=0, we need (p,0) allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) and (0,0). So p must be 2 or 0.\n\nThus p ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow we have constraints linking n to a via c20: (n,a) allowed: (1,3), (1,0). So n must be 1, and a must be 3 or 0. Since we found n ∈ {1,3}, but c20 restricts n=1 only (since allowed pairs are (1,3) and (1,0)). So n must be 1. So n=1.\n\nThus from c13 (m=0,n=1) is allowed? Check c13 allowed pairs: (0,1) is allowed. Yes.\n\nThus n=1.\n\nNow c14: (n,o) = (1,o) must be allowed. Allowed pairs for n=1: from c14 list: (1,3) is allowed. Also (3,0) etc but those have first component 3. So only (1,3) appears. So o must be 3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (3,p) must be allowed. Allowed pairs for o=3: from c15 list: (3,2) and (3,3). Also (0,2) etc but those have first component 0. So allowed (3,2) and (3,3). So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nBut we also have c16: (p,m) = (p,0) must be allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) and (0,0). So p must be 2 or 0. Intersection with {2,3} yields p=2 (since p=3 not allowed). So p=2.\n\nThus p=2, o=3, n=1, m=0.\n\nThus the c4 component (m,n,o,p) is uniquely determined: (0,1,3,2). So c4 component tuple enumeration is a single tuple (0,1,3,2). That is consistent with constraints c13-c16 and bridge c19 (m=0). So the enumeration is supported and unique (i.e., determined). So we can say assessment \"supported\". The enumeration is not underdetermined; it's determined.\n\nNow we need to check the rest of the variables. We have determined m,n,o,p uniquely. Now we need to solve for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l.\n\nWe have constraints linking a,b,c,d and n,a etc. Let's incorporate n=1, a unknown but constrained by c20: (n,a) = (1,a) allowed: (1,3) and (1,0). So a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus a ∈ {0,3}.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So we need to consider possible (d,a) given a ∈ {0,3}.\n\nCase 1: a=3. Then allowed (d,3) pairs from c4: (2,3) and (1,3) and (3,3). So d ∈ {2,1,3}.\n\nCase 2: a=0. Then allowed (d,0) pairs from c4: (3,0) is allowed. Also (0,1) is not relevant because a=0. So only (3,0) is allowed. So d=3.\n\nThus if a=0, then d=3 uniquely.\n\nNow c1: (a,b) allowed pairs. For each a, we need possible b.\n\nc1 allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1).\n\n- If a=3: possible b values: from pairs where a=3: (3,2) => b=2; (3,1) => b=1. Also (2,3) has a=2, not relevant; (0,2) a=0; (1,1) a=1; (0,1) a=0. So for a=3, b ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- If a=0: possible b values: (0,2) => b=2; (0,1) => b=1. So b ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus b ∈ {1,2} regardless of a, but specific combos depend on a.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs. For each b ∈ {1,2}, we need possible c.\n\nc2 allowed pairs: (2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3).\n\n- For b=2: allowed c values: from (2,1) => c=1; also (3,2) is b=3, not relevant; (0,2) b=0; (0,0) b=0; (3,1) b=3; (0,3) b=0. So only (2,1) gives b=2. So if b=2, then c=1.\n\n- For b=1: any allowed pairs with b=1? None. The list includes b=2,0,3,0,3,0. So b=1 does not appear. So b cannot be 1. Therefore b must be 2. Good.\n\nThus b=2.\n\nGiven b=2, from c1 we have a must be 3 (since a=3 yields b=2). Check c1: (a,b) = (3,2) is allowed. Also (a,b) = (2,3) is not relevant because a=2 not allowed. So a=3.\n\nThus a=3, b=2.\n\nNow from c2 with b=2, we have c=1 (as above). So c=1.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs. c=1, so allowed (1,d) pairs from c3: (1,2) is allowed. Also (2,2) etc but c=2 not relevant. So only (1,2) yields d=2. So d=2.\n\nThus we have a=3,b=2,c=1,d=2.\n\nNow check c4: (d,a) = (2,3) is allowed. Yes, (2,3) is in c4 allowed pairs. So consistent.\n\nNow we have a,b,c,d solved.\n\nNow we need to solve for e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l.\n\nWe have c17: (b,e) allowed: (2,0) or (0,2). Since b=2, we need (2,0). So e=0.\n\nThus e=0.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed: (0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1). Since e=0, allowed pairs with e=0: (0,1) => f=1; (0,2) => f=2. Also (0,?) any others? (0,?) only those two. So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) allowed: (1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0). So for each possible f, we need possible g.\n\n- If f=1: allowed g values: from (1,3) => g=3; (1,1) => g=1; (1,0) => g=0. Also (0,1) is f=0 not relevant; (2,0) f=2; (2,3) f=2. So f=1 yields g ∈ {3,1,0}.\n\n- If f=2: allowed g values: (2,3) => g=3; (2,0) => g=0. Also (0,1) not relevant; (1,3) f=1; (1,1) f=1; (1,0) f=1. So f=2 yields g ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus possible (f,g) combos:\n- (1,3), (1,1), (1,0), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). So for each g, possible h.\n\n- If g=3: allowed h: (3,2) => h=2; (3,1) => h=1; (3,0) => h=0. So h ∈ {2,1,0}.\n\n- If g=2: allowed h: (2,1) => h=1. Also (2,?) any others? (2,?) only (2,1). So h=1.\n\n- If g=1: allowed h: (1,0) => h=0. Also (1,?) any others? (1,0) only. So h=0.\n\n- If g=0: allowed h: (0,0) => h=0.\n\nThus possible (g,h) combos:\n- g=3 => h∈{2,1,0}\n- g=2 => h=1\n- g=1 => h=0\n- g=0 => h=0\n\nNow c8: (h,e) allowed: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). Since e=0, we need allowed (h,0) pairs. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) => h=2; (0,3) has second component 3, not relevant; (1,2) second component 2; (1,1) second component 1; (2,2) second component 2; (2,1) second component 1. So only (2,0) yields h=2. So h must be 2.\n\nThus h=2.\n\nNow from c8, we have h=2, e=0, and (2,0) is allowed. So consistent.\n\nNow we have h=2, so we need to find g such that (g,h) = (g,2) is allowed. From c7 allowed (g,h) pairs: (3,2) is allowed; (3,1) not; (2,1) not; (1,0) not; (0,0) not; (3,0) not. So only (g=3,h=2) is allowed. So g must be 3.\n\nThus g=3.\n\nNow from c6, (f,g) = (f,3) must be allowed. Allowed (f,g) pairs: (1,3) => f=1; (2,3) => f=2. Also (0,1) etc not relevant. So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow from c5, (e,f) = (0,f) must be allowed. Allowed (0,f): (0,1) => f=1; (0,2) => f=2. So f ∈ {1,2}. So both possibilities are allowed.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for (f,g): (1,3) or (2,3). Both satisfy c5, c6, c7, c8.\n\nNow we need to consider c18: (f,i) allowed: (1,0) or (2,1). So if f=1, then i=0; if f=2, then i=1.\n\nThus i is determined: i = 0 if f=1; i = 1 if f=2.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c9: (i,j) allowed pairs. For each possible i, we need possible j.\n\nc9 allowed pairs: (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So:\n\n- If i=0: allowed j ∈ {3,2,0}.\n- If i=1: allowed j ∈ {1}.\n\nThus if f=1 => i=0 => j ∈ {3,2,0}. If f=2 => i=1 => j=1.\n\nNow c10: (j,k) allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for each possible j, we need possible k.\n\n- If j=3: allowed k ∈ {0,2} (from (3,0) and (3,2)).\n- If j=2: allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) is j=0, not relevant; (0,1) j=0; (1,0) j=1; (1,3) j=1; (3,0) j=3; (3,2) j=3). Actually for j=2, we need pairs where first component is 2. The list includes (0,2) (first component 0), (0,1) (0), (1,0) (1), (1,3) (1), (3,0) (3), (3,2) (3). So there is no pair with j=2. So j cannot be 2. So j cannot be 2.\n\n- If j=0: allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) and (0,1)). Also (0,?) maybe (0,?) only those two.\n\n- If j=1: allowed k ∈ {0,3} (from (1,0) and (1,3)).\n\nThus j possibilities: 3,0,1. But j=2 is invalid. So from i=0, j ∈ {3,2,0} but j=2 invalid, so j ∈ {3,0}. So if f=1 (i=0), j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nIf f=2 (i=1), j=1.\n\nNow we need to also satisfy c19: (j,m) allowed: (3,0) or (0,0). Since m=0, we need (j,0) allowed. So allowed pairs: (3,0) and (0,0). So j must be 3 or 0. So j ∈ {3,0}. This matches the possibilities from i=0 case (j ∈ {3,0}) and also matches j=1 case? No, j=1 is not allowed because (1,0) is not in c19 allowed pairs. So j cannot be 1. Therefore the case f=2 (i=1, j=1) is invalid because j=1 violates c19. So f cannot be 2. Therefore f must be 1.\n\nThus f=1.\n\nThus i = 0 (since f=1 => i=0). j must be either 3 or 0 (allowed by c9 and c19). So j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c10: (j,k) allowed pairs. For each j:\n\n- If j=3: allowed k ∈ {0,2} (from (3,0) and (3,2)).\n- If j=0: allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) and (0,1)).\n\nThus possible (j,k):\n- (3,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,1)\n\nNow c11: (k,l) allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So for each possible k, we need possible l.\n\n- If k=0: allowed l ∈ {2,0}? Actually from c11 list: (0,2) => l=2; (1,0) is k=1; (1,3) k=1; (3,3) k=3; (2,0) k=2; (3,2) k=3. So only (0,2) yields l=2 for k=0. So if k=0, then l=2.\n\n- If k=2: allowed l ∈ {0,3}? Actually (2,0) => l=0; (3,2) is k=3; (3,3) is k=3; (1,3) is k=1; (1,0) is k=1; (0,2) is k=0. So only (2,0) yields l=0 for k=2. So if k=2, then l=0.\n\n- If k=1: allowed l ∈ {3,0} (from (1,3) and (1,0)). So if k=1, l ∈ {3,0}.\n\n- If k=3: allowed l ∈ {3,2} (from (3,3) and (3,2)). So if k=3, l ∈ {3,2}.\n\nThus we have possible (k,l) combos.\n\nNow c12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1). Since i=0, we need (l,0) allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) => l=2; (??) any others? (1,1) second component 1; (3,3) second component 3; (2,3) second component 3; (2,2) second component 2; (3,1) second component 1. So only (2,0) yields l=2. So l must be 2.\n\nThus l=2.\n\nNow from c12, (l,i) = (2,0) is allowed. Good.\n\nThus l=2.\n\nNow we need to find k such that (k,l) = (k,2) is allowed per c11. c11 allowed pairs: (0,2) => k=0; (3,2) => k=3; (2,0) => k=2 but l=0 not 2; (1,3) => l=3; (1,0) => l=0; (3,3) => l=3. So only (0,2) and (3,2) produce l=2. So k ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus k=0 or k=3.\n\nNow we need to check c10: (j,k) allowed pairs. For each possible j (3 or 0) and k (0 or 3), we need to see if the pair is allowed.\n\nList of allowed (j,k) pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So allowed combos:\n\n- (j=3,k=0) is allowed.\n- (j=3,k=2) is allowed.\n- (j=0,k=2) is allowed.\n- (j=0,k=1) is allowed.\n- (j=1,k=0) is allowed.\n- (j=1,k=3) is allowed.\n\nThus (j,k) possibilities are limited to those combos.\n\nNow we have j ∈ {3,0}, k ∈ {0,3}. Let's enumerate possibilities:\n\n- j=3, k=0: allowed? Yes (3,0) is allowed.\n- j=3, k=3: allowed? (3,3) is not in allowed list. So (3,3) is not allowed.\n- j=0, k=0: allowed? (0,0) is not in allowed list. So not allowed.\n- j=0, k=3: allowed? (0,3) is not in allowed list. So not allowed.\n\nThus the only allowed combination is (j=3,k=0). So j=3, k=0.\n\nThus we have j=3, k=0, l=2, i=0, f=1, g=3, h=2, e=0, b=2, a=3, c=1, d=2, m=0, n=1, o=3, p=2.\n\nNow we need to verify all constraints to ensure consistency.\n\nLet's list all variables:\n\na=3\nb=2\nc=1\nd=2\ne=0\nf=1\ng=3\nh=2\ni=0\nj=3\nk=0\nl=2\nm=0\nn=1\no=3\np=2\n\nNow check each constraint:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c1 allowed pairs.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) is in c2 allowed pairs.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (1,2) allowed? Yes (1,2) is in c3 allowed pairs.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c4 allowed pairs.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is in c5 allowed pairs.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c6 allowed pairs.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c7 allowed pairs.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c8 allowed pairs.\n\nc9 (i,j) = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) is in c9 allowed pairs.\n\nc10 (j,k) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c10 allowed pairs.\n\nc11 (k,l) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c11 allowed pairs.\n\nc12 (l,i) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c12 allowed pairs.\n\nc13 (m,n) = (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is in c13 allowed pairs.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c14 allowed pairs.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c15 allowed pairs.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c16 allowed pairs.\n\nc17 (b,e) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c17 allowed pairs.\n\nc18 (f,i) = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is in c18 allowed pairs.\n\nc19 (j,m) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c19 allowed pairs.\n\nc20 (n,a) = (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c20 allowed pairs.\n\nThus all constraints satisfied. So the assignment is a complete solution.\n\nNow the c4 component tuple enumeration: The c4 component includes variables m,n,o,p. In this solution, they are (0,1,3,2). Are there any other possible assignments? We need to check if there are multiple solutions. The constraints seem to uniquely determine all variables as we found. But we need to verify if there could be alternative solutions. Let's explore if any alternative assignments exist.\n\nWe solved a,b,c,d uniquely. Then e=0 forced f=1 (since f=2 was invalid due to c19). So f=1 uniquely. Then g=3 uniquely. Then h=2 uniquely. Then i=0 uniquely. Then j must be 3 (since j=0 would conflict with c10 and c11? Let's see if j=0 could be possible with some alternative assignments for k,l? Let's explore alternative possibilities.\n\nWe found j ∈ {3,0} from c9 and c19. Let's see if j=0 could be possible with some other assignments for k,l etc.\n\nWe have constraints:\n\n- i=0 (since f=1 => i=0). So i=0.\n\n- c9: (i,j) = (0,j) allowed: j ∈ {3,2,0}. But c19 restricts j ∈ {3,0}. So j ∈ {3,0}. So j could be 0.\n\n- c10: (j,k) allowed pairs. For j=0, allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) and (0,1)). So k ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- c11: (k,l) allowed pairs. For each possible k, we need l.\n\n- c12: (l,i) = (l,0) allowed: only (2,0) yields l=2. So l must be 2.\n\nThus from c12, l=2.\n\nNow c11: (k,l) = (k,2) allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 2: from c11 list: (0,2) => k=0; (3,2) => k=3. Also (??) any other? (2,0) second component 0; (1,3) second component 3; (1,0) second component 0; (3,3) second component 3. So only (0,2) and (3,2) produce l=2. So k ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus k ∈ {0,3}.\n\nNow combine with c10: For j=0, allowed k ∈ {2,1}. Intersection with k ∈ {0,3} yields no possible k. So j=0 is impossible. Therefore j must be 3.\n\nThus j=3 uniquely.\n\nNow c10: (j,k) = (3,k) allowed: allowed k ∈ {0,2} (from (3,0) and (3,2)). So k ∈ {0,2}.\n\nBut we also have from c11 that k ∈ {0,3} (to get l=2). Intersection yields k=0 only. So k=0 uniquely.\n\nThus k=0.\n\nNow c11: (k,l) = (0,2) allowed, yields l=2 as required.\n\nThus all variables are uniquely determined. So the solution is unique.\n\nThus the c4 component tuple enumeration is a single tuple (0,1,3,2). So the enumeration is determined (i.e., not underdetermined). So the assessment should be \"supported\" (i.e., the enumeration is consistent with constraints). The phrase \"supported or underdetermined\" suggests we can choose \"supported\" if the enumeration is consistent and determined. So we should set assessment to \"supported\". The basis_refs list includes all constraints IDs. That seems appropriate.\n\nNow we need to produce the envelope. The envelope includes \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\". The \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\". The \"payload\" includes \"report\". The \"report\" includes \"assessment\": \"supported\". \"basis_refs\": list of all constraint IDs. \"target\": resource ID etc. Then \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\".\n\nThus we need to output exactly that JSON, with assessment \"supported\". The basis_refs list includes all constraint IDs from c1 to c20. The order in the template includes all IDs. We need to ensure we include all IDs exactly as in template. The template includes:\n\n\"basis_refs\": [\n  \"c1\",\n  \"c2\",\n  \"c3\",\n  \"c4\",\n  \"c5\",\n  \"c6\",\n  \"c7\",\n  \"c8\",\n  \"c9\",\n  \"c10\",\n  \"c11\",\n  \"c12\",\n  \"c13\",\n  \"c14\",\n  \"c15\",\n  \"c16\",\n  \"c17\",\n  \"c18\",\n  \"c19\",\n  \"c20\"\n]\n\nWe need to keep that exactly. The \"target\" includes \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1. The \"read_refs\" also includes same resource_id with revision 1 and state_revision 1.\n\nThus the envelope is basically the same as template, except maybe we need to adjust \"assessment\". The template had \"assessment\": \"supported\". So we keep that.\n\nNow the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" But we haven't added candidate tuples. However, perhaps candidate tuples are part of the \"payload\"? But the template does not show them. Possibly the envelope expects a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? Or maybe inside \"report\"? The instruction might be generic for a different envelope format. But we only have this envelope template. So we will output the envelope as is, with assessment \"supported\". There's no candidate tuples. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" Perhaps we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? But the envelope template does not have it. However, the instruction might be generic for a different scenario. But we must follow the envelope template.\n\nThus we will output the envelope exactly as template, with assessment \"supported\". There's no need to add candidate tuples because they are not present. The instruction might be irrelevant or we might need to include candidate tuples but not shown. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" It might be that the envelope template includes a \"candidate_tuples\" field but it's omitted in the snippet. But the snippet shows only \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". There's no \"candidate_tuples\". So perhaps we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field under \"payload\"? Or maybe under \"report\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to include candidate tuples. But we need to decide where to put them.\n\nLet's examine the problem description: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output a JSON envelope. The envelope template is provided. It includes fields. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope template includes a field \"candidate_tuples\" but it's not shown in the snippet. However, the snippet includes \"payload\": { \"report\": { \"assessment\": \"...\", \"basis_refs\": [...], \"target\": {...} } }. There's no \"candidate_tuples\". Could be that the \"report\" also includes \"candidate_tuples\" but it's omitted. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe we need to add \"candidate_tuples\" inside \"report\". But the template does not show it. However, the instruction says \"matching the supplied template\". That could mean we need to match the overall shape but we can add fields not shown? Usually \"matching the supplied template\" means we need to produce a JSON that matches the structure, but we can fill in values. It doesn't necessarily forbid adding extra fields. But we need to be careful: The envelope may be consumed by a system that expects certain fields. Adding extra fields might be okay. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit those fields. It doesn't say we cannot add other fields. But we need to ensure we include candidate tuples. But we need to decide what candidate tuples to include. The \"candidate tuples\" likely refer to the enumeration for the c4 component (the tuple(s) that satisfy constraints). Since we have a unique tuple (0,1,3,2), we could include that as a candidate tuple. Possibly the envelope expects a \"candidate_tuples\" field under \"report\". Or maybe under \"payload\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit candidate tuples. So we need to include candidate tuples. But the template doesn't show it, but we can add it.\n\nThus we need to produce a JSON envelope that includes a \"candidate_tuples\" field. Where? Possibly inside \"payload.report\". The \"report\" object currently has \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". We can add \"candidate_tuples\" as a field inside \"report\". That would be consistent with typical structure: a report might contain candidate tuples.\n\nThus we will produce:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [ ... ],\n      \"target\": { ... },\n      \"candidate_tuples\": [\n        {\n          \"component\": \"c4\",\n          \"variables\": {\n            \"m\": 0,\n            \"n\": 1,\n            \"o\": 3,\n            \"p\": 2\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [ ... ],\n  \"recipients\": [ \"shared-integrator\" ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\"\n}\n\nBut the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" It does not mention component labeling. But candidate tuples could be a list of tuples for the c4 component. The format is not defined. We need to guess. The envelope template is not detailed. However, the problem description says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" That is part of the answer requirements. So we need to enumerate the satisfying tuple for c4 component (m,n,o,p). So candidate tuples likely should contain that enumeration.\n\nThus we need to include candidate tuples that enumerate the satisfying tuple(s) for c4 component. Since we have a unique tuple, we can include that tuple as a candidate.\n\nThus we need to add a field \"candidate_tuples\" inside \"payload.report\". The format could be an array of objects, each representing a tuple. The problem description says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So the tuple is (m,n,o,p) = (0,1,3,2). So we could represent it as an array of integers [0,1,3,2] or as a map from variable names to values. The instruction says \"in variable order\". The variable order for component c4 is [\"m\",\"n\",\"o\",\"p\"] (as per components.c4). So we could output a tuple as an array of values in that order: [0,1,3,2].\n\nThus candidate_tuples could be something like:\n\n\"candidate_tuples\": [\n  [0,1,3,2]\n]\n\nAlternatively, could be a list of objects mapping variable names to values. But the instruction says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So likely they expect a list of tuples (arrays). So we can include \"candidate_tuples\": [[0,1,3,2]].\n\nThus we will add \"candidate_tuples\": [[0,1,3,2]] inside \"payload.report\". That would satisfy the requirement.\n\nNow we need to ensure the JSON is valid. We'll produce the envelope with all fields as described.\n\nNow we need to check if any other fields need editing. The \"assessment\" is \"supported\". The \"basis_refs\" list is as given. The \"target\" is as given. The \"read_refs\" is as given. The \"recipients\" is [\"shared-integrator\"]. The \"reply_to\" is null. The \"schema_version\" is 1. The \"sender\" is \"c3-reviewer\". The \"message_id\" is \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\". The \"intent\" is \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\". The \"problem_id\" is \"csp-6582\".\n\nThus the envelope is:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\n        \"c1\",\n        \"c2\",\n        \"c3\",\n        \"c4\",\n        \"c5\",\n        \"c6\",\n        \"c7\",\n        \"c8\",\n        \"c9\",\n        \"c10\",\n        \"c11\",\n        \"c12\",\n        \"c13\",\n        \"c14\",\n        \"c15\",\n        \"c16\",\n        \"c17\",\n        \"c18\",\n        \"c19\",\n        \"c20\"\n      ],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      },\n      \"candidate_tuples\": [\n        [0,1,3,2]\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\"\n}\n\nNow we need to ensure we haven't introduced any syntax errors. The JSON is valid.\n\nNow we need to check if any other constraints need to be considered. The problem says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below.\" That likely means we need to produce a solution (assignment) for a through p satisfying all constraints. However, the envelope we are producing is a report of evidence, not the solution. But the envelope is the output we need to return. The envelope includes candidate tuples for c4 component. It doesn't include the full assignment. But the problem description says \"Solve the finite constraint task... Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So the envelope is the output. The envelope includes a report of evidence about c4 component enumeration. It does not need to include the full assignment. But the problem also says \"Give one complete assignment for a through p satisfying all twenty constraints.\" That is part of the answer requirements. However, the envelope we produce is a report, not the assignment. But perhaps the envelope includes the assignment in candidate tuples? But candidate tuples only include c4 component tuple. The assignment for a-p is not included. However, the problem description might be generic; the envelope is the output of the CSP solver. The envelope includes a report about c4 component enumeration. The envelope may be used to communicate the solution. But the envelope does not contain the full assignment. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we are not supposed to edit the assignment. The assignment is not part of the envelope. So perhaps the envelope is not meant to include the full assignment; the assignment is part of the problem solving but not required to be output. The envelope is just a report.\n\nBut the problem description says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" The envelope template is a report of evidence. So maybe the expected output is a report that includes the c4 component tuple enumeration. The assignment is not needed in the envelope. The \"candidate tuples\" likely refer to the enumeration for c4 component. So we are done.\n\nThus we will output the envelope as above.\n\nNow we need to ensure we follow the instruction \"Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\" So we will output the JSON exactly as plain text.\n\nThus final answer: the JSON envelope.\n\nWe should double-check the order of fields in the envelope. The template includes fields in a specific order: \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\", \"schema_version\", \"sender\". We need to preserve that order. The \"payload\" includes \"report\". Inside \"report\", we have \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". We added \"candidate_tuples\". The order of fields inside \"report\" may not matter, but we can keep \"candidate_tuples\" after \"target\". The template order is \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". We can keep that order and add \"candidate_tuples\" after \"target\". That should be fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure the JSON is valid with proper quoting. We'll output the JSON exactly.\n\nOne more thing: The \"candidate_tuples\" field might need to be an array of objects or arrays. The problem says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So we could include a tuple as an array of values. So \"candidate_tuples\": [[0,1,3,2]] is appropriate.\n\nNow we need to ensure we haven't missed any other constraints that could produce alternative solutions. Let's double-check the possibility of f=2 case. We earlier eliminated f=2 because it leads to i=1, j=1, which violates c19. But let's double-check if there could be any alternative path where f=2 but j is not 1? Actually c18: (f,i) allowed: (1,0) or (2,1). So if f=2, i=1. Then c9: (i,j) allowed: i=1 => j must be 1 (only (1,1) allowed). So j=1. Then c19: (j,m) allowed: (3,0) or (0,0). j=1 not allowed. So f=2 is impossible. So f=1 is forced.\n\nNow check if any alternative for g/h? We found g=3, h=2 forced because h must be 2 from c8. Let's verify if any alternative for h could be something else. c8 allowed (h,e) pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). Since e=0, we need (h,0) allowed. Only (2,0) yields h=2. So h=2 forced. Then c7: (g,h) allowed: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). Since h=2, we need (g,2) allowed. Only (3,2) yields g=3. So g=3 forced. So f,g,h are forced.\n\nNow check if any alternative for e? e=0 forced by c17 (b=2 => e=0). So e forced.\n\nNow check if any alternative for b? b forced to 2 earlier. Let's double-check if b could be something else. c1 allowed (a,b) pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). c2 allowed (b,c) pairs: (2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3). c20 allowed (n,a) pairs: (1,3), (1,0). c4 allowed (d,a) pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). c17 allowed (b,e) pairs: (2,0), (0,2). So b must be either 2 or 0. Let's explore b=0 possibility.\n\nIf b=0, then from c17, (b,e) = (0,2) => e=2. Then c5 (e,f) allowed: (0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1). Since e=2, allowed f: (2,2) => f=2; (2,1) => f=1. So f ∈ {2,1}. But we earlier eliminated f=2 due to c19. Let's see if f=1 is possible with b=0.\n\nIf f=1, then c6 (f,g) allowed: (1,3), (1,1), (1,0). So g ∈ {3,1,0}. c7 (g,h) allowed: depends on g. c8 (h,e) allowed: e=2, so (h,2) allowed. Let's see c8 allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So for e=2, we need (h,2) allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 2: (1,2) => h=1; (2,2) => h=2. So h ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed pairs: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). So for each possible g, we need h.\n\nLet's explore possibilities systematically for b=0 case.\n\nCase b=0, e=2.\n\nNow c1: (a,b) = (a,0) allowed? c1 allowed pairs: (3,2) not; (2,3) not; (3,1) not; (0,2) not; (1,1) not; (0,1) not. So there is no allowed pair with b=0. Indeed c1 allowed pairs list: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). None have b=0. So b cannot be 0. So b=2 is forced. Good.\n\nThus b=2 forced.\n\nThus all variables are uniquely determined as we found.\n\nThus the solution is unique.\n\nThus the c4 component tuple enumeration is unique: (0,1,3,2). So the report is \"supported\". No need for CHALLENGE.\n\nThus we will output the envelope as described.\n\nNow we need to ensure we include \"candidate_tuples\". The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit candidate tuples. But we need to decide what candidate tuples to include. The problem description says \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" So for component c4, the satisfying tuple is (0,1,3,2). So we include that.\n\nThus we will include \"candidate_tuples\": [[0,1,3,2]].\n\nNow we need to ensure we haven't introduced any syntax errors. Let's write the JSON with proper commas.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\n        \"c1\",\n        \"c2\",\n        \"c3\",\n        \"c4\",\n        \"c5\",\n        \"c6\",\n        \"c7\",\n        \"c8\",\n        \"c9\",\n        \"c10\",\n        \"c11\",\n        \"c12\",\n        \"c13\",\n        \"c14\",\n        \"c15\",\n        \"c16\",\n        \"c17\",\n        \"c18\",\n        \"c19\",\n        \"c20\"\n      ],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      },\n      \"candidate_tuples\": [\n        [0,1,3,2]\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\"\n}\n\nNow we need to ensure that we haven't introduced any trailing commas. The JSON is valid.\n\nNow we need to output exactly that JSON, no extra text. So we will output the JSON as the final answer.\n\nThus the final answer is the JSON above.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet. The envelope template is given with fields. We need to produce a JSON envelope matching the template. The envelope includes \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\", etc. The payload contains a report with assessment, basis_refs, target etc. The task is to independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19 (['j', 'm']). Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. No accepted peer proposal is available; report that state without inventing one.\n\nWe need to produce the envelope. The envelope template is given with placeholders? It includes a particular structure: \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\", \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\", \"payload\": {\"report\": {\"assessment\": \"supported\", \"basis_refs\": [...], \"target\": {...}} }, etc. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" But the envelope template doesn't have candidate tuples. Perhaps we need to include candidate tuples? Actually, the \"payload\" contains a \"report\" object; inside that we have \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". There's no candidate tuples in this envelope. But maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" But the envelope template given doesn't have candidate tuples. Possibly the \"payload\" also contains a \"candidate_tuples\" field? Or maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside the report? The problem description: \"Independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19 (['j', 'm']). Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\"\n\nSo the envelope likely should include a \"candidate_tuples\" field somewhere. But the given envelope template does not have it. Perhaps the envelope template is just a skeleton, and we need to fill in the fields. The instructions say \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So we need to output a JSON that matches the template, but we may need to adjust fields based on the assessment. It says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to produce a JSON that includes those fields possibly. The template includes \"assessment\": \"supported\". But we might need to change to \"underdetermined\" or \"supported\". The basis_refs list is given. Should we edit it? Probably not needed. But we need to check the c4 component tuple enumeration against constraints and bridge c19.\n\nThus the primary task is to examine the c4 component tuple enumeration (i.e., the values for variables in component c4: m, n, o, p). The constraints c13-c16 involve those variables. c13: (m, n) allowed pairs: [ [0,1], [2,2], [0,3], [3,0], [2,1], [2,0] ]. c14: (n, o) allowed: [ [1,3], [3,2], [3,0], [0,1], [2,0], [0,3] ]. c15: (o, p) allowed: [ [3,2], [0,2], [3,3], [0,1], [2,1], [1,1] ]. c16: (p, m) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,3], [0,0], [1,2], [1,3], [2,3] ].\n\nAlso bridge constraints: c19 is a bridge between component c3 and c4, variables j and m. c19 allowed pairs: [ [3,0], [0,0] ]. So j (in component c3) and m (in component c4) must be either (3,0) or (0,0). So m is either 0 (if j=0) or 0 (if j=3) or maybe m=0 always? Actually both options have m=0. So m must be 0. So from bridge c19 we deduce m = 0.\n\nThus we need to see if the c4 component tuple enumeration (i.e., all possible assignments for m,n,o,p satisfying constraints c13-c16) is consistent with m=0 from bridge c19. The c4 component tuple enumeration presumably is the set of all tuples (m,n,o,p) that satisfy constraints c13-c16 (and maybe also other constraints linking to other components? c4 also appears in constraints c4 (d,a) and c20 (n,a) but those are separate. c4 component is m,n,o,p. c4 is also a constraint between d and a (c4: (d,a) allowed pairs). That is separate. The c4 component is distinct from constraint c4. The component c4 includes variables m,n,o,p.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all tuples (m,n,o,p) that satisfy c13-c16. Then check if any of those have m=0 as required by bridge c19. If all have m=0, then the enumeration is consistent. If some have m != 0, then there is a conflict.\n\nWe need to check if the enumeration is \"supported\" (i.e., consistent with constraints) or \"underdetermined\" (i.e., ambiguous). The instructions: \"Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined.\" So we need to assess whether the c4 component tuple enumeration is fully determined (i.e., unique) or underdetermined (multiple possibilities). Or maybe \"supported\" means the enumeration is consistent with constraints. \"Underdetermined\" means there are multiple possibilities. The phrasing \"supported or underdetermined\" suggests we need to choose between these two. Usually in CSP, \"supported\" means the enumeration is consistent and possibly unique? But \"underdetermined\" means there are multiple possibilities.\n\nWe need to \"independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19 (['j', 'm']).\" So we need to verify that the enumeration includes only tuples that satisfy c13-c16 and also satisfy the bridge constraint c19 (i.e., m=0). The enumeration is presumably given somewhere? But we have not been given any enumeration. The problem statement says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below.\" So we need to solve the CSP: find a complete assignment for a through p satisfying all constraints. Then we need to \"independently check the c4 component tuple enumeration against the public constraints and bridge constraint c19\". So maybe we need to produce the enumeration for c4 component (i.e., the values of m,n,o,p) as part of solving the CSP. The envelope template includes \"assessment\" and \"basis_refs\". There's no \"candidate_tuples\". But maybe the envelope is expected to contain a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to include candidate tuples in the envelope. The envelope template is given but maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside the \"report\"? Or maybe inside \"payload\"? The template didn't show candidate tuples, but we can add them.\n\nBut the instructions say \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\" So we must output JSON that matches the template. The template is a JSON object with fields: \"intent\", \"message_id\", \"payload\", \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\", \"schema_version\", \"sender\". Inside payload, there is \"report\" with fields \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". There's no candidate_tuples. So maybe the envelope is just a report of evidence, not including candidate tuples. The instruction about editing candidate tuples might refer to a different part of the protocol not shown. But we only have this envelope template. So perhaps we just need to output the envelope with assessment \"supported\" (or \"underdetermined\") and basis_refs as given. The candidate tuples might be part of the \"report\" but not shown. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope template is incomplete and we need to include candidate tuples. But the envelope template is the one we need to match. It doesn't have candidate tuples. So maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? But that would deviate from the template.\n\nWe need to interpret the instructions. The problem: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we must produce a JSON envelope that matches the template. The template includes fields. The envelope is a report of evidence. The \"assessment\" field currently is \"supported\". We might need to adjust to \"underdetermined\" if appropriate. The \"basis_refs\" list includes all constraints IDs. That seems fine.\n\nWe need to check the c4 component tuple enumeration against constraints. So we need to solve the CSP to find assignments for a-p. Then we need to check the enumeration for c4 component (m,n,o,p). We need to see if it's consistent with constraints and bridge c19. Then we need to produce a report with assessment \"supported\" if consistent and maybe unique? Or \"underdetermined\" if there are multiple possibilities. The instruction says \"Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined.\" So if we find an issue (e.g., enumeration violates constraints), we would use CHALLENGE. But we haven't been given any enumeration to check; we need to compute it. So we need to compute the enumeration and see if it is consistent.\n\nThus the primary task is to solve the CSP. Let's do that.\n\nWe have 16 variables: a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints:\n\n- c1: (a,b) allowed pairs: [ [3,2], [2,3], [3,1], [0,2], [1,1], [0,1] ]\n- c2: (b,c) allowed: [ [2,1], [0,2], [3,2], [0,0], [3,1], [0,3] ]\n- c3: (c,d) allowed: [ [1,2], [2,2], [2,1], [3,2], [0,1], [0,0] ]\n- c4: (d,a) allowed: [ [2,3], [2,2], [3,0], [1,3], [3,3], [0,1] ]\n- c5: (e,f) allowed: [ [0,1], [1,1], [2,2], [0,2], [3,3], [2,1] ]\n- c6: (f,g) allowed: [ [1,3], [2,3], [0,1], [2,0], [1,1], [1,0] ]\n- c7: (g,h) allowed: [ [3,2], [3,1], [2,1], [1,0], [0,0], [3,0] ]\n- c8: (h,e) allowed: [ [2,0], [1,1], [2,2], [2,1], [0,3], [1,2] ]\n- c9: (i,j) allowed: [ [0,3], [0,2], [0,0], [1,1], [3,2], [2,3] ]\n- c10: (j,k) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,2], [1,0], [0,1], [1,3], [3,2] ]\n- c11: (k,l) allowed: [ [0,2], [1,3], [3,3], [2,0], [3,2], [1,0] ]\n- c12: (l,i) allowed: [ [2,0], [1,1], [3,3], [2,3], [2,2], [3,1] ]\n- c13: (m,n) allowed: [ [0,1], [2,2], [0,3], [3,0], [2,1], [2,0] ]\n- c14: (n,o) allowed: [ [1,3], [3,2], [3,0], [0,1], [2,0], [0,3] ]\n- c15: (o,p) allowed: [ [3,2], [0,2], [3,3], [0,1], [2,1], [1,1] ]\n- c16: (p,m) allowed: [ [2,0], [3,3], [0,0], [1,2], [1,3], [2,3] ]\n\nBridge constraints:\n- c17: (b,e) allowed: [ [2,0], [0,2] ]\n- c18: (f,i) allowed: [ [1,0], [2,1] ]\n- c19: (j,m) allowed: [ [3,0], [0,0] ]\n- c20: (n,a) allowed: [ [1,3], [1,0] ]\n\nThus we have a CSP with constraints linking variables across components.\n\nWe need to find a complete assignment for a through p satisfying all constraints. Then we need to check the c4 component tuple enumeration (i.e., the values of m,n,o,p) against constraints and bridge c19. So we need to solve the CSP, then examine the c4 component.\n\nWe need to produce a report of evidence. The envelope includes assessment and basis_refs. The candidate tuples might be part of the report? But the envelope template does not include candidate tuples. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" Possibly the envelope template is incomplete and we need to include candidate tuples in the \"payload\"? Let's read the envelope template fully:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\n        \"c1\",\n        \"c2\",\n        ...\n      ],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\"\n}\n\nThus the envelope is a report. There's no candidate tuples. So maybe the candidate tuples are part of the \"payload\" but not shown. Or maybe we need to output a different envelope that includes candidate tuples, but the instruction says \"matching the supplied template\". So we must match exactly the structure, but we can edit fields. The candidate tuples might be part of the \"report\" but not present in the template. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So perhaps the envelope template originally includes candidate tuples, but they are omitted in the snippet. But we have to follow the template. It might be that the \"payload\" field includes a \"report\" object that may have additional fields like \"candidate_tuples\". The template shows only \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". But we can add \"candidate_tuples\" if needed, but that would deviate from the template. However, the instruction says \"matching the supplied template\". That could be interpreted as the overall shape, not necessarily exact fields. But likely they want us to keep the same fields and not add extra ones. But the instruction about editing candidate tuples suggests there is a field for candidate tuples somewhere. Maybe the \"payload\" field also contains a \"candidate_tuples\" key, but it's not shown in the template because it's optional. The envelope template might be incomplete; we need to fill in the missing fields. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to produce a report that includes a \"candidate_tuples\" field. The \"assessment\" and \"basis_refs\" are already there. So we need to add \"candidate_tuples\". But the template does not show it, but we can include it as part of \"payload.report\". That would be a deviation but likely acceptable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the \"payload\" is a map that includes \"report\" and also \"candidate_tuples\". The template might have omitted \"candidate_tuples\" for brevity. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to include candidate tuples. The \"assessment\" currently is \"supported\". We need to decide if it's \"supported\" or \"underdetermined\". Let's solve the CSP.\n\nWe have 16 variables and constraints. Let's attempt to solve manually or using reasoning. The constraints are fairly restrictive. Let's try to solve systematically.\n\nWe have constraints linking a,b,c,d in a cycle: c1 (a,b), c2 (b,c), c3 (c,d), c4 (d,a). Also c20 (n,a) and c17 (b,e) and c18 (f,i) and c19 (j,m) and c5-c8 (e,f,g,h cycle) and c9-c12 (i,j,k,l cycle). So we have two cycles: (a,b,c,d) and (e,f,g,h) and (i,j,k,l) and (m,n,o,p) with bridge constraints linking them: c17 links b-e, c18 links f-i, c19 links j-m, c20 links n-a.\n\nThus we have a combined graph.\n\nWe need to find assignments for all variables. Let's denote domains: each variable ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nWe have constraints:\n\nc1: allowed (a,b) pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1). So possible (a,b):\n- a=3,b=2\n- a=2,b=3\n- a=3,b=1\n- a=0,b=2\n- a=1,b=1\n- a=0,b=1\n\nc2: (b,c) allowed: (2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3). So possible (b,c):\n- b=2,c=1\n- b=0,c=2\n- b=3,c=2\n- b=0,c=0\n- b=3,c=1\n- b=0,c=3\n\nc3: (c,d) allowed: (1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (3,2), (0,1), (0,0). So possible (c,d):\n- c=1,d=2\n- c=2,d=2\n- c=2,d=1\n- c=3,d=2\n- c=0,d=1\n- c=0,d=0\n\nc4: (d,a) allowed: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So possible (d,a):\n- d=2,a=3\n- d=2,a=2\n- d=3,a=0\n- d=1,a=3\n- d=3,a=3\n- d=0,a=1\n\nc20: (n,a) allowed: (1,3), (1,0). So possible (n,a):\n- n=1,a=3\n- n=1,a=0\n\nc17: (b,e) allowed: (2,0), (0,2). So possible (b,e):\n- b=2,e=0\n- b=0,e=2\n\nc5: (e,f) allowed: (0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1). So possible (e,f):\n- e=0,f=1\n- e=1,f=1\n- e=2,f=2\n- e=0,f=2\n- e=3,f=3\n- e=2,f=1\n\nc6: (f,g) allowed: (1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0). So possible (f,g):\n- f=1,g=3\n- f=2,g=3\n- f=0,g=1\n- f=2,g=0\n- f=1,g=1\n- f=1,g=0\n\nc7: (g,h) allowed: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). So possible (g,h):\n- g=3,h=2\n- g=3,h=1\n- g=2,h=1\n- g=1,h=0\n- g=0,h=0\n- g=3,h=0\n\nc8: (h,e) allowed: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). So possible (h,e):\n- h=2,e=0\n- h=1,e=1\n- h=2,e=2\n- h=2,e=1\n- h=0,e=3\n- h=1,e=2\n\nc18: (f,i) allowed: (1,0), (2,1). So possible (f,i):\n- f=1,i=0\n- f=2,i=1\n\nc9: (i,j) allowed: (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So possible (i,j):\n- i=0,j=3\n- i=0,j=2\n- i=0,j=0\n- i=1,j=1\n- i=3,j=2\n- i=2,j=3\n\nc10: (j,k) allowed: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So possible (j,k):\n- j=3,k=0\n- j=0,k=2\n- j=1,k=0\n- j=0,k=1\n- j=1,k=3\n- j=3,k=2\n\nc11: (k,l) allowed: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So possible (k,l):\n- k=0,l=2\n- k=1,l=3\n- k=3,l=3\n- k=2,l=0\n- k=3,l=2\n- k=1,l=0\n\nc12: (l,i) allowed: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1). So possible (l,i):\n- l=2,i=0\n- l=1,i=1\n- l=3,i=3\n- l=2,i=3\n- l=2,i=2\n- l=3,i=1\n\nc19: (j,m) allowed: (3,0), (0,0). So possible (j,m):\n- j=3,m=0\n- j=0,m=0\n\nThus m must be 0, and j must be either 3 or 0.\n\nc13: (m,n) allowed: (0,1), (2,2), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1), (2,0). Since m=0, we need (0,n) allowed. Allowed pairs with first component 0: (0,1) and (0,3). So n must be 1 or 3.\n\nThus n ∈ {1,3}.\n\nc14: (n,o) allowed: (1,3), (3,2), (3,0), (0,1), (2,0), (0,3). So for each possible n, we need allowed o.\n\nc15: (o,p) allowed: (3,2), (0,2), (3,3), (0,1), (2,1), (1,1).\n\nc16: (p,m) allowed: (2,0), (3,3), (0,0), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). Since m=0, we need (p,0) allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) and (0,0). So p must be 2 or 0.\n\nThus p ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow we have constraints linking n to a via c20: (n,a) allowed: (1,3), (1,0). So n must be 1, and a must be 3 or 0. Since we found n ∈ {1,3}, but c20 restricts n=1 only (since allowed pairs are (1,3) and (1,0)). So n must be 1. So n=1.\n\nThus from c13 (m=0,n=1) is allowed? Check c13 allowed pairs: (0,1) is allowed. Yes.\n\nThus n=1.\n\nNow c14: (n,o) = (1,o) must be allowed. Allowed pairs for n=1: from c14 list: (1,3) is allowed. Also (3,0) etc but those have first component 3. So only (1,3) appears. So o must be 3.\n\nThus o=3.\n\nNow c15: (o,p) = (3,p) must be allowed. Allowed pairs for o=3: from c15 list: (3,2) and (3,3). Also (0,2) etc but those have first component 0. So allowed (3,2) and (3,3). So p ∈ {2,3}.\n\nBut we also have c16: (p,m) = (p,0) must be allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) and (0,0). So p must be 2 or 0. Intersection with {2,3} yields p=2 (since p=3 not allowed). So p=2.\n\nThus p=2, o=3, n=1, m=0.\n\nThus the c4 component (m,n,o,p) is uniquely determined: (0,1,3,2). So c4 component tuple enumeration is a single tuple (0,1,3,2). That is consistent with constraints c13-c16 and bridge c19 (m=0). So the enumeration is supported and unique (i.e., determined). So we can say assessment \"supported\". The enumeration is not underdetermined; it's determined.\n\nNow we need to check the rest of the variables. We have determined m,n,o,p uniquely. Now we need to solve for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l.\n\nWe have constraints linking a,b,c,d and n,a etc. Let's incorporate n=1, a unknown but constrained by c20: (n,a) = (1,a) allowed: (1,3) and (1,0). So a ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus a ∈ {0,3}.\n\nNow c4: (d,a) allowed pairs: (2,3), (2,2), (3,0), (1,3), (3,3), (0,1). So we need to consider possible (d,a) given a ∈ {0,3}.\n\nCase 1: a=3. Then allowed (d,3) pairs from c4: (2,3) and (1,3) and (3,3). So d ∈ {2,1,3}.\n\nCase 2: a=0. Then allowed (d,0) pairs from c4: (3,0) is allowed. Also (0,1) is not relevant because a=0. So only (3,0) is allowed. So d=3.\n\nThus if a=0, then d=3 uniquely.\n\nNow c1: (a,b) allowed pairs. For each a, we need possible b.\n\nc1 allowed pairs: (3,2), (2,3), (3,1), (0,2), (1,1), (0,1).\n\n- If a=3: possible b values: from pairs where a=3: (3,2) => b=2; (3,1) => b=1. Also (2,3) has a=2, not relevant; (0,2) a=0; (1,1) a=1; (0,1) a=0. So for a=3, b ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- If a=0: possible b values: (0,2) => b=2; (0,1) => b=1. So b ∈ {2,1}.\n\nThus b ∈ {1,2} regardless of a, but specific combos depend on a.\n\nNow c2: (b,c) allowed pairs. For each b ∈ {1,2}, we need possible c.\n\nc2 allowed pairs: (2,1), (0,2), (3,2), (0,0), (3,1), (0,3).\n\n- For b=2: allowed c values: from (2,1) => c=1; also (3,2) is b=3, not relevant; (0,2) b=0; (0,0) b=0; (3,1) b=3; (0,3) b=0. So only (2,1) gives b=2. So if b=2, then c=1.\n\n- For b=1: any allowed pairs with b=1? None. The list includes b=2,0,3,0,3,0. So b=1 does not appear. So b cannot be 1. Therefore b must be 2. Good.\n\nThus b=2.\n\nGiven b=2, from c1 we have a must be 3 (since a=3 yields b=2). Check c1: (a,b) = (3,2) is allowed. Also (a,b) = (2,3) is not relevant because a=2 not allowed. So a=3.\n\nThus a=3, b=2.\n\nNow from c2 with b=2, we have c=1 (as above). So c=1.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) allowed pairs. c=1, so allowed (1,d) pairs from c3: (1,2) is allowed. Also (2,2) etc but c=2 not relevant. So only (1,2) yields d=2. So d=2.\n\nThus we have a=3,b=2,c=1,d=2.\n\nNow check c4: (d,a) = (2,3) is allowed. Yes, (2,3) is in c4 allowed pairs. So consistent.\n\nNow we have a,b,c,d solved.\n\nNow we need to solve for e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l.\n\nWe have c17: (b,e) allowed: (2,0) or (0,2). Since b=2, we need (2,0). So e=0.\n\nThus e=0.\n\nNow c5: (e,f) allowed: (0,1), (1,1), (2,2), (0,2), (3,3), (2,1). Since e=0, allowed pairs with e=0: (0,1) => f=1; (0,2) => f=2. Also (0,?) any others? (0,?) only those two. So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) allowed: (1,3), (2,3), (0,1), (2,0), (1,1), (1,0). So for each possible f, we need possible g.\n\n- If f=1: allowed g values: from (1,3) => g=3; (1,1) => g=1; (1,0) => g=0. Also (0,1) is f=0 not relevant; (2,0) f=2; (2,3) f=2. So f=1 yields g ∈ {3,1,0}.\n\n- If f=2: allowed g values: (2,3) => g=3; (2,0) => g=0. Also (0,1) not relevant; (1,3) f=1; (1,1) f=1; (1,0) f=1. So f=2 yields g ∈ {3,0}.\n\nThus possible (f,g) combos:\n- (1,3), (1,1), (1,0), (2,3), (2,0).\n\nNow c7: (g,h) allowed: (3,2), (3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (0,0), (3,0). So for each g, possible h.\n\n- If g=3: allowed h: (3,2) => h=2; (3,1) => h=1; (3,0) => h=0. So h ∈ {2,1,0}.\n\n- If g=2: allowed h: (2,1) => h=1. Also (2,?) any others? (2,?) only (2,1). So h=1.\n\n- If g=1: allowed h: (1,0) => h=0. Also (1,?) any others? (1,0) only. So h=0.\n\n- If g=0: allowed h: (0,0) => h=0.\n\nThus possible (g,h) combos:\n- g=3 => h∈{2,1,0}\n- g=2 => h=1\n- g=1 => h=0\n- g=0 => h=0\n\nNow c8: (h,e) allowed: (2,0), (1,1), (2,2), (2,1), (0,3), (1,2). Since e=0, we need allowed (h,0) pairs. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) => h=2; (0,3) has second component 3, not relevant; (1,2) second component 2; (1,1) second component 1; (2,2) second component 2; (2,1) second component 1. So only (2,0) yields h=2. So h must be 2.\n\nThus h=2.\n\nNow from c8, we have h=2, e=0, and (2,0) is allowed. So consistent.\n\nNow we have h=2, so we need to find g such that (g,h) = (g,2) is allowed. From c7 allowed (g,h) pairs: (3,2) is allowed; (3,1) not; (2,1) not; (1,0) not; (0,0) not; (3,0) not. So only (g=3,h=2) is allowed. So g must be 3.\n\nThus g=3.\n\nNow from c6, (f,g) = (f,3) must be allowed. Allowed (f,g) pairs: (1,3) => f=1; (2,3) => f=2. Also (0,1) etc not relevant. So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow from c5, (e,f) = (0,f) must be allowed. Allowed (0,f): (0,1) => f=1; (0,2) => f=2. So f ∈ {1,2}. So both possibilities are allowed.\n\nThus we have two possibilities for (f,g): (1,3) or (2,3). Both satisfy c5, c6, c7, c8.\n\nNow we need to consider c18: (f,i) allowed: (1,0) or (2,1). So if f=1, then i=0; if f=2, then i=1.\n\nThus i is determined: i = 0 if f=1; i = 1 if f=2.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c9: (i,j) allowed pairs. For each possible i, we need possible j.\n\nc9 allowed pairs: (0,3), (0,2), (0,0), (1,1), (3,2), (2,3). So:\n\n- If i=0: allowed j ∈ {3,2,0}.\n- If i=1: allowed j ∈ {1}.\n\nThus if f=1 => i=0 => j ∈ {3,2,0}. If f=2 => i=1 => j=1.\n\nNow c10: (j,k) allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So for each possible j, we need possible k.\n\n- If j=3: allowed k ∈ {0,2} (from (3,0) and (3,2)).\n- If j=2: allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) is j=0, not relevant; (0,1) j=0; (1,0) j=1; (1,3) j=1; (3,0) j=3; (3,2) j=3). Actually for j=2, we need pairs where first component is 2. The list includes (0,2) (first component 0), (0,1) (0), (1,0) (1), (1,3) (1), (3,0) (3), (3,2) (3). So there is no pair with j=2. So j cannot be 2. So j cannot be 2.\n\n- If j=0: allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) and (0,1)). Also (0,?) maybe (0,?) only those two.\n\n- If j=1: allowed k ∈ {0,3} (from (1,0) and (1,3)).\n\nThus j possibilities: 3,0,1. But j=2 is invalid. So from i=0, j ∈ {3,2,0} but j=2 invalid, so j ∈ {3,0}. So if f=1 (i=0), j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nIf f=2 (i=1), j=1.\n\nNow we need to also satisfy c19: (j,m) allowed: (3,0) or (0,0). Since m=0, we need (j,0) allowed. So allowed pairs: (3,0) and (0,0). So j must be 3 or 0. So j ∈ {3,0}. This matches the possibilities from i=0 case (j ∈ {3,0}) and also matches j=1 case? No, j=1 is not allowed because (1,0) is not in c19 allowed pairs. So j cannot be 1. Therefore the case f=2 (i=1, j=1) is invalid because j=1 violates c19. So f cannot be 2. Therefore f must be 1.\n\nThus f=1.\n\nThus i = 0 (since f=1 => i=0). j must be either 3 or 0 (allowed by c9 and c19). So j ∈ {3,0}.\n\nNow we need to satisfy c10: (j,k) allowed pairs. For each j:\n\n- If j=3: allowed k ∈ {0,2} (from (3,0) and (3,2)).\n- If j=0: allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) and (0,1)).\n\nThus possible (j,k):\n- (3,0)\n- (3,2)\n- (0,2)\n- (0,1)\n\nNow c11: (k,l) allowed pairs: (0,2), (1,3), (3,3), (2,0), (3,2), (1,0). So for each possible k, we need possible l.\n\n- If k=0: allowed l ∈ {2,0}? Actually from c11 list: (0,2) => l=2; (1,0) is k=1; (1,3) k=1; (3,3) k=3; (2,0) k=2; (3,2) k=3. So only (0,2) yields l=2 for k=0. So if k=0, then l=2.\n\n- If k=2: allowed l ∈ {0,3}? Actually (2,0) => l=0; (3,2) is k=3; (3,3) is k=3; (1,3) is k=1; (1,0) is k=1; (0,2) is k=0. So only (2,0) yields l=0 for k=2. So if k=2, then l=0.\n\n- If k=1: allowed l ∈ {3,0} (from (1,3) and (1,0)). So if k=1, l ∈ {3,0}.\n\n- If k=3: allowed l ∈ {3,2} (from (3,3) and (3,2)). So if k=3, l ∈ {3,2}.\n\nThus we have possible (k,l) combos.\n\nNow c12: (l,i) allowed pairs: (2,0), (1,1), (3,3), (2,3), (2,2), (3,1). Since i=0, we need (l,0) allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 0: (2,0) => l=2; (??) any others? (1,1) second component 1; (3,3) second component 3; (2,3) second component 3; (2,2) second component 2; (3,1) second component 1. So only (2,0) yields l=2. So l must be 2.\n\nThus l=2.\n\nNow from c12, (l,i) = (2,0) is allowed. Good.\n\nThus l=2.\n\nNow we need to find k such that (k,l) = (k,2) is allowed per c11. c11 allowed pairs: (0,2) => k=0; (3,2) => k=3; (2,0) => k=2 but l=0 not 2; (1,3) => l=3; (1,0) => l=0; (3,3) => l=3. So only (0,2) and (3,2) produce l=2. So k ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus k=0 or k=3.\n\nNow we need to check c10: (j,k) allowed pairs. For each possible j (3 or 0) and k (0 or 3), we need to see if the pair is allowed.\n\nList of allowed (j,k) pairs: (3,0), (0,2), (1,0), (0,1), (1,3), (3,2). So allowed combos:\n\n- (j=3,k=0) is allowed.\n- (j=3,k=2) is allowed.\n- (j=0,k=2) is allowed.\n- (j=0,k=1) is allowed.\n- (j=1,k=0) is allowed.\n- (j=1,k=3) is allowed.\n\nThus (j,k) possibilities are limited to those combos.\n\nNow we have j ∈ {3,0}, k ∈ {0,3}. Let's enumerate possibilities:\n\n- j=3, k=0: allowed? Yes (3,0) is allowed.\n- j=3, k=3: allowed? (3,3) is not in allowed list. So (3,3) is not allowed.\n- j=0, k=0: allowed? (0,0) is not in allowed list. So not allowed.\n- j=0, k=3: allowed? (0,3) is not in allowed list. So not allowed.\n\nThus the only allowed combination is (j=3,k=0). So j=3, k=0.\n\nThus we have j=3, k=0, l=2, i=0, f=1, g=3, h=2, e=0, b=2, a=3, c=1, d=2, m=0, n=1, o=3, p=2.\n\nNow we need to verify all constraints to ensure consistency.\n\nLet's list all variables:\n\na=3\nb=2\nc=1\nd=2\ne=0\nf=1\ng=3\nh=2\ni=0\nj=3\nk=0\nl=2\nm=0\nn=1\no=3\np=2\n\nNow check each constraint:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c1 allowed pairs.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (2,1) allowed? Yes (2,1) is in c2 allowed pairs.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (1,2) allowed? Yes (1,2) is in c3 allowed pairs.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c4 allowed pairs.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is in c5 allowed pairs.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c6 allowed pairs.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c7 allowed pairs.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c8 allowed pairs.\n\nc9 (i,j) = (0,3) allowed? Yes (0,3) is in c9 allowed pairs.\n\nc10 (j,k) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c10 allowed pairs.\n\nc11 (k,l) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c11 allowed pairs.\n\nc12 (l,i) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c12 allowed pairs.\n\nc13 (m,n) = (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is in c13 allowed pairs.\n\nc14 (n,o) = (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c14 allowed pairs.\n\nc15 (o,p) = (3,2) allowed? Yes (3,2) is in c15 allowed pairs.\n\nc16 (p,m) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c16 allowed pairs.\n\nc17 (b,e) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c17 allowed pairs.\n\nc18 (f,i) = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is in c18 allowed pairs.\n\nc19 (j,m) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c19 allowed pairs.\n\nc20 (n,a) = (1,3) allowed? Yes (1,3) is in c20 allowed pairs.\n\nThus all constraints satisfied. So the assignment is a complete solution.\n\nNow the c4 component tuple enumeration: The c4 component includes variables m,n,o,p. In this solution, they are (0,1,3,2). Are there any other possible assignments? We need to check if there are multiple solutions. The constraints seem to uniquely determine all variables as we found. But we need to verify if there could be alternative solutions. Let's explore if any alternative assignments exist.\n\nWe solved a,b,c,d uniquely. Then e=0 forced f=1 (since f=2 was invalid due to c19). So f=1 uniquely. Then g=3 uniquely. Then h=2 uniquely. Then i=0 uniquely. Then j must be 3 (since j=0 would conflict with c10 and c11? Let's see if j=0 could be possible with some alternative assignments for k,l? Let's explore alternative possibilities.\n\nWe found j ∈ {3,0} from c9 and c19. Let's see if j=0 could be possible with some other assignments for k,l etc.\n\nWe have constraints:\n\n- i=0 (since f=1 => i=0). So i=0.\n\n- c9: (i,j) = (0,j) allowed: j ∈ {3,2,0}. But c19 restricts j ∈ {3,0}. So j ∈ {3,0}. So j could be 0.\n\n- c10: (j,k) allowed pairs. For j=0, allowed k ∈ {2,1} (from (0,2) and (0,1)). So k ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- c11: (k,l) allowed pairs. For each possible k, we need l.\n\n- c12: (l,i) = (l,0) allowed: only (2,0) yields l=2. So l must be 2.\n\nThus from c12, l=2.\n\nNow c11: (k,l) = (k,2) allowed. Allowed pairs with second component 2: from c11 list: (0,2) => k=0; (3,2) => k=3. Also (??) any other? (2,0) second component 0; (1,3) second component 3; (1,0) second component 0; (3,3) second component 3. So only (0,2) and (3,2) produce l=2. So k ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus k ∈ {0,3}.\n\nNow combine with c10: For j=0, allowed k ∈ {2,1}. Intersection with k ∈ {0,3} yields no possible k. So j=0 is impossible. Therefore j must be 3.\n\nThus j=3 uniquely.\n\nNow c10: (j,k) = (3,k) allowed: allowed k ∈ {0,2} (from (3,0) and (3,2)). So k ∈ {0,2}.\n\nBut we also have from c11 that k ∈ {0,3} (to get l=2). Intersection yields k=0 only. So k=0 uniquely.\n\nThus k=0.\n\nNow c11: (k,l) = (0,2) allowed, yields l=2 as required.\n\nThus all variables are uniquely determined. So the solution is unique.\n\nThus the c4 component tuple enumeration is a single tuple (0,1,3,2). So the enumeration is determined (i.e., not underdetermined). So the assessment should be \"supported\" (i.e., the enumeration is consistent with constraints). The phrase \"supported or underdetermined\" suggests we can choose \"supported\" if the enumeration is consistent and determined. So we should set assessment to \"supported\". The basis_refs list includes all constraints IDs. That seems appropriate.\n\nNow we need to produce the envelope. The envelope includes \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\". The \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\". The \"payload\" includes \"report\". The \"report\" includes \"assessment\": \"supported\". \"basis_refs\": list of all constraint IDs. \"target\": resource ID etc. Then \"problem_id\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\".\n\nThus we need to output exactly that JSON, with assessment \"supported\". The basis_refs list includes all constraint IDs from c1 to c20. The order in the template includes all IDs. We need to ensure we include all IDs exactly as in template. The template includes:\n\n\"basis_refs\": [\n  \"c1\",\n  \"c2\",\n  \"c3\",\n  \"c4\",\n  \"c5\",\n  \"c6\",\n  \"c7\",\n  \"c8\",\n  \"c9\",\n  \"c10\",\n  \"c11\",\n  \"c12\",\n  \"c13\",\n  \"c14\",\n  \"c15\",\n  \"c16\",\n  \"c17\",\n  \"c18\",\n  \"c19\",\n  \"c20\"\n]\n\nWe need to keep that exactly. The \"target\" includes \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6582\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1. The \"read_refs\" also includes same resource_id with revision 1 and state_revision 1.\n\nThus the envelope is basically the same as template, except maybe we need to adjust \"assessment\". The template had \"assessment\": \"supported\". So we keep that.\n\nNow the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" But we haven't added candidate tuples. However, perhaps candidate tuples are part of the \"payload\"? But the template does not show them. Possibly the envelope expects a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? Or maybe inside \"report\"? The instruction might be generic for a different envelope format. But we only have this envelope template. So we will output the envelope as is, with assessment \"supported\". There's no candidate tuples. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" Perhaps we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload\"? But the envelope template does not have it. However, the instruction might be generic for a different scenario. But we must follow the envelope template.\n\nThus we will output the envelope exactly as template, with assessment \"supported\". There's no need to add candidate tuples because they are not present. The instruction might be irrelevant or we might need to include candidate tuples but not shown. However, the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" It might be that the envelope template includes a \"candidate_tuples\" field but it's omitted in the snippet. But the snippet shows only \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". There's no \"candidate_tuples\". So perhaps we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field under \"payload\"? Or maybe under \"report\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to include candidate tuples. But we need to decide where to put them.\n\nLet's examine the problem description: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output a JSON envelope. The envelope template is provided. It includes fields. The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe the envelope template includes a field \"candidate_tuples\" but it's not shown in the snippet. However, the snippet includes \"payload\": { \"report\": { \"assessment\": \"...\", \"basis_refs\": [...], \"target\": {...} } }. There's no \"candidate_tuples\". Could be that the \"report\" also includes \"candidate_tuples\" but it's omitted. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe we need to add \"candidate_tuples\" inside \"report\". But the template does not show it. However, the instruction says \"matching the supplied template\". That could mean we need to match the overall shape but we can add fields not shown? Usually \"matching the supplied template\" means we need to produce a JSON that matches the structure, but we can fill in values. It doesn't necessarily forbid adding extra fields. But we need to be careful: The envelope may be consumed by a system that expects certain fields. Adding extra fields might be okay. But the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit those fields. It doesn't say we cannot add other fields. But we need to ensure we include candidate tuples. But we need to decide what candidate tuples to include. The \"candidate tuples\" likely refer to the enumeration for the c4 component (the tuple(s) that satisfy constraints). Since we have a unique tuple (0,1,3,2), we could include that as a candidate tuple. Possibly the envelope expects a \"candidate_tuples\" field under \"report\". Or maybe under \"payload\"? The instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit candidate tuples. So we need to include candidate tuples. But the template doesn't show it, but we can add it.\n\nThus we need to produce a JSON envelope that includes a \"candidate_tuples\" field. Where? Possibly inside \"payload.report\". The \"report\" object currently has \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". We can add \"candidate_tuples\" as a field inside \"report\". That would be consistent with typical structure: a report might contain candidate tuples.\n\nThus we will produce:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-6582-c3-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [ ... ],\n      \"target\": { ... },\n      \"candidate_tuples\": [\n        {\n          \"component\": \"c4\",\n          \"variables\": {\n            \"m\": 0,\n            \"n\": 1,\n            \"o\": 3,\n            \"p\": 2\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6582\",\n  \"read_refs\": [ ... ],\n  \"recipients\": [ \"shared-integrator\" ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c3-reviewer\"\n}\n\nBut the instruction says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" It does not mention component labeling. But candidate tuples could be a list of tuples for the c4 component. The format is not defined. We need to guess. The envelope template is not detailed. However, the problem description says \"Enu